I have implemented request logging in your Next.js application by adding a `proxy.ts` file (which is the new convention for `middleware` in recent Next.js 16 canary versions). 

The `proxy.ts` file logs every request to the console in the `[HTTP_METHOD] URL` format using the Edge Runtime.

Here is the implementation that was added to the root directory:

```typescript
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server';

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  console.log(`[${request.method}] ${request.url}`);
  return NextResponse.next();
}
```

The app builds successfully without any deprecation warnings. Every incoming request (including pages, API routes, and static assets) will now be logged to your terminal.

